From 7809edf5a886b844bfc7da7d4d7290ae7a1ff8a0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: BellSoft <info@bell-sw.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 19:45:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add the prototype JDK and NIK ecosystems of the vendor to
 osvecosystem and semantic

Standalone vendor runtimes are covered by two prototype OSV ecosystems
(liberica-osv/ECOSYSTEM.md): "<JDK family>:<java major>" (package name =
variant "jdk", "jre", "jdk-lite", ...; versions in product notation
"21.0.2+14", "21+37", "8u402+7") and "<NIK family>:<nik major>-<java major>"
(package name = bundle "core", "standard", "full"; versions "23.1.0+1").
Neither is in the osv-schema constants yet.

* osvecosystem: EcosystemVendorJDK and EcosystemVendorNIK are valid
  ecosystems; the values are the osv-internal module's.
* semantic: versions of both families are parsed and compared by the
  jdkver and nikver packages of github.com/bell-sw/osv-internal (new
  dependency): numeric order on every component, then on the build; the
  raw runtime forms "21.0.2+14-LTS" and "1.8.0_402-b07" are accepted too.
  The three kinds of update release are valid versions: PSU (21.0.12+10,
  8u502+9), CSPU (21.0.12.1+1, 8u504+1) and CPU (21.0.9.0.1+1, 8u481).
* osvlocal: a CPU carries the security fixes of the PSU it is released
  with while advisories name the PSU as the fixed version, so the upper
  bound of a range is compared through the optional CompareUpperBoundStr
  of a version: 21.0.9.0.1+1 is not affected by an advisory fixed in
  21.0.10+10, and is by one fixed in 21.0.11+11. Other ecosystems are
  compared as before.
